**Data types
 1. Any- super type all datatypes
 2. Built-in data types (numBer, string, null , undefined etc)
 3. User defined (array, enum, class, interface etc)
 
 
 A variable initialized with undefined means that the variable has no value or object assigned to it while null 
 means that the variable has been set to an object whose value is undefined.
 
 A variable, by definition, is “a named space in the memory” that stores values. 
 
 **variable declaration
    var variableName: dataType = value;
    var variableName: dataType;
	var variableName= value;
	var variableName; // dataType is any
**type assertions
      changing variable from one dataType to another.
ex:-var str = '1';
    var str2:number = <number> <any> str   //str is now of type number 	  

this is not called as type casting, since this data type conversion is not during run time, it's compile time change.

**Inferred Typing
     same as dynamic keyword in c#
ex- var num = 2;    // data type inferred as  number  (datatype of num is not defined,once  it 2 is assigned to it means it's data type will v number)

**Variable Scope
1. Global variable:-  Global variables are declared outside the programming constructs.
2. Class scope:- called fields in a class. it supports  static properties too.
3. Local scope:- local variable.
ex:
var global_num = 12          //global variable 
class Numbers { 
   num_val = 13;             //class variable 
   static sval = 10;         //static field 
   
   storeNum():void { 
      var local_num = 14;    //local variable 
   } 
} 

	 
